itƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;jbPr79zAERhpTG9kVDSaP-Q_-ZQJRWb1dsJb5_x7e0gƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;ODg9pVslKr9BSGKgHxqGjk2YFejBIhSgV_rbgFdd4Q4ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;TaN_dNwtmfHhkHoVh7jD6oUxRE0UsBjBekjPRmGZd6gƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;aqM5McdvIvVmfBGpy66heQOjoE6o0q72171-U7If8wgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;33tHSzwolsjcaKBxbtxWKIV0z12NLr2PBZsNXvAhIbgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$9363674a-d3b7-456c-92e5-a93bdcd1d802ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;66KLtTRvwQrs7FBEQxmYhtV-9vT9qWsMAPf9HBecPPoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkyJ{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"Draft repair only: fix near-duplicate \"reconfiguration\" in closing paragraph; no source or assignment replay needed.","summary":"The manuscript is well-sourced and analytically coherent but carries two copy-edit near-duplicate defects flagged by the standards gate. One is material (the word \"reconfiguration\" used twice in the same closing paragraph); the other is marginal but warrants review. Both are bounded repairs that do not require replay from sources or assignment.","requiredRepairs":["In the final paragraph, the distinctive word 'reconfiguration' appears twice in close proximity: 'Spain's speed of reconfiguration against France's speed of recognition' and 'Whether Spain's collective reconfiguration can contain Messi's decision-making.' Replace the second instance with alternative phrasing that preserves the meaning without repeating the word â€” for example, recast to focus on Spain's capacity to reshape or reorganise â€” so that the paragraph's closing question does not echo its own opening clause.","Review the lede near-duplicate flag (copy-edit gate detected proximity between 'against France' in sentence one and 'Franceâ€™s pressing' in the MbappÃ© sentence of the same paragraph). The two sentences make genuinely different points, so if the editor judges the flag a false positive it may be cleared; if tightening is possible without losing either fact, it should be applied."],"report":"## Source and Attribution Audit\n\nThe primary source is Conor Boland's analysis published in The Conversation on 2026-07-17 at https://theconversation.com/geometry-football-and-the-world-cup-final-287792. The author is correctly identified by name in the body (\"Conor Boland, writing in The Conversation\"), satisfying attribution requirements. The disclosure line in the source text (\"Conor Boland does not work for, consultâ€¦\") confirms this is the bylined author, not a subject of reporting.\n\nSupporting context is drawn from a second Conversation article by Steve Georgakis (https://theconversation.com/tactically-complete-spain-vs-an-inspired-messi-led-argentina-who-will-win-the-world-cup-final-287372). This source is used only for independently warranted comparative facts: both sides unbeaten over seven matches; Spain conceded one goal; Oyarzabal on five goals; Argentina the Copa AmÃ©rica and World Cup holders; Spain's 2010 triumph; Argentina's pursuit of back-to-back crowns not achieved in six decades. All of these claims are present in the admitted source text. Attribution is provided by inline link, which is acceptable for supporting context.\n\nExternal links that appear inside the primary source text â€” to Nature player-tracking research, to the AP News goal report, to the PLOS ONE passing-network paper, and to the Basketball Hall of Fame page on Tex Winter â€” are carried through the manuscript correctly. They originate in Boland's analysis and are not independently sourced by the writer model.\n\nNo facts are invented. The MbappÃ© quote (\"a lack of communication\" in France's pressing) is sourced to the Boland article, which itself links to Reuters â€” an appropriate chain.\n\n## Headline and Lede Faithfulness\n\nThe headline \"Spain's Geometry Meets Messi's Magic in World Cup Final Showdown\" preserves the source article's core organizing terms (geometry, World Cup final) and adds a forward-looking framing from the supporting context (Messi). \"Showdown\" is mild promotional language but does not misstate the event's stakes or claim certainty about outcome. The word \"magic\" edges toward characterization beyond what the Boland primary source licenses (Boland does not discuss Messi), but the Georgakis supporting source does address Messi's role, and the headline covers the article as a whole. The headline does not assert a winner or manufacture conflict beyond what the sources support.\n\nThe lede correctly identifies the event (Spain v Argentina at MetLife Stadium), names the date, and introduces the geometric analysis angle before the first subheader. Word count for the lede is within range.\n\n## Structural Review\n\nThree H2 sections build the argument sequentially: \"Forcing choices\" (the mechanics of passing triangles), \"A pitch of probabilities\" (temporal and spatial control, backed by research links), and \"Spain, Argentina, and Sunday's question\" (how the tactical frame applies to the final). Sections do not reintroduce the event; each continues from the previous. No section is devoted to a single source. The closing paragraph opens outward without predicting an outcome. No rhetorical questions appear. The manuscript avoids \"will\" outside a source context.\n\nThe demand-lens H2 warning (no subheading naturally signals \"geometry football cup final\") is noted. The article discusses geometry throughout but no H2 uses the term. Since the commission says exact keyword copy is not required and the analytical content fully expresses the lens, this warning is editorially minor and does not block publication once the near-duplicate repairs are made.\n\n## Near-Duplicate Analysis\n\n**First flag (lede paragraph):** The copy-edit gate detected proximity between \"against France\" (sentence 1) and \"France's pressing\" (sentence 3, the MbappÃ© sentence). These two sentences make genuinely different factual claims â€” one describes Spain's passing geometry; the other quotes MbappÃ© on his team's pressing failure. The shared reference to \"France\" does not constitute lexical repetition of a phrase. This flag is likely a gate false positive. The editor should confirm or tighten at discretion; no mandatory change is required.\n\n**Second flag (final paragraph) â€” material:** The word \"reconfiguration\" appears twice in the same paragraph: first in \"Spain's speed of reconfiguration against France's speed of recognition\" (attributing Boland's analysis) and again in \"Whether Spain's collective reconfiguration can contain Messi's decision-making.\" This is a genuine copy-edit defect. \"Reconfiguration\" is a distinctive, uncommon word; its repetition within six sentences creates an echo that the standards gate correctly identifies. The second use must be reworded to preserve the closing question's meaning without repeating the noun.\n\n## Claim Traceability\n\nEvery consequential factual assertion is traceable to a licensed claim in the research packet. The 774-word count sits within the 600â€“850 range specified in the commission. No passage-plan claims from unrelated sources (Cabo Verde, VAR controversies, Western Sahara, racism) have been introduced. Context sources are correctly excluded.\n\n## Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe defects are confined to the writer-model layer. The assignment is well-formed, the sources are adequate for the article's scope, and the deterministic gate found no blocking errors â€” only copy-edit warnings. The near-duplicate \"reconfiguration\" repetition and the marginal lede flag are prose-level issues arising in the draft itself, not compelled by thin sourcing or an unsupported assignment. A targeted draft repair addresses the failure without requiring replay from sources or instructions."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;MDLJfaxA2LcTbO08Bkf4KYkvt6S0QtwuDVnVca6Gx5Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;szI-hlvEH6ZWCYFU2zC4q5JXqF3AzKDqM1QQntgtAiEƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;0TDpVOOVRWuUw8_Cgt2JubcwQ9CVaH7eBpPBOwWkkVgƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;CUm8_rCcdSLSG_YPzb91n_ZeqxGxXi5hoWj_eVldGq0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;zFSeKIXlR4uVAF0IoA3vUL_5ol-EWbsjwDRHUMGRafMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;K__wXS8e0SMJ3BmzwKdhJRDMXOiD4nwf0e8g5I-E6_sƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg182.752ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;2c9lva6JUilwAQKGwO3SBg99GmulteztZ3gkStCPVZ0ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;MP50ZdEMLvAirWQb8shVfkNH7Y_hlwhoCNyEA5XovoIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;mVOoDdt6Ww